-- title:
--   Each retained node's prediction was detected before it was chosen
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. The tree is built level by level from candidate records: each candidate is a generated map attached to a parent node together with a norm-one functional chosen after the map, and every candidate, including those that later receive coefficient zero, consumes a global ordinal $n$ with budget $2^{-(n+9)}$. Consider the tree built by the candidate stages, with anchor $a$, terminal sequence $b_j$ and ultrafilter $\mathcal U$.
--
--   1. **Prefix parameter.** The final actual parameter of a retained node $v$, transported to the exact history recorded when $v$ was chosen.
--   2. **Prefix prediction.** The prediction limit of that history at this parameter.
--   3. **Final prediction limit.** The same point described by the final tree: the weak limit of the ideal cutoffs at $v$.
--
--   Both describe the same point. It lies in the detector set fixed before the map at $v$ was chosen, so
--   $$
--   \varphi_v\bigl(g_v(a)-\Lambda_v\bigr)\ge1-\beta(\mathrm{label}\,v).
--   $$
